+## Opt-in verified HTTPS (dependent proposal)
+
+An event `request` or `download` can declare `"tls": {"ca_file": "certs/root.pem"}`.
+The CA path is relative to that plugin's directory, at most 128 bytes;
+absolute paths, any `..` sequence, duplicate separators and backslashes are
+rejected. The PEM file is capped at 8KB and must contain a certificate envelope.
+The TLS implementation parses the certificate and verifies the peer.
+
+A present `tls` field opts in even when its value is null, empty or of the wrong
+type. An invalid opt-in fails the request and retains the event; it never falls
+back to insecure HTTP. Verified requests require HTTPS with a nonempty
+authority, a fresh transport and an SNTP callback observed after the current
+drain starts its synchronization attempt. A saved clock floor or an earlier
+SNTP `COMPLETED` status does not qualify. A direct verified HTTP call requires
+the internal callback proof, which expires within 30 seconds under both the
+monotonic and wall clocks. A new failed drain attempt invalidates prior proof.
+SNTP here does not authenticate UTC cryptographically.
+
+Userinfo and query/fragment text within the authority are rejected. Write
+`https://example.test/?token=...`, with a slash before any query; raw control
+characters, NUL and backslashes are rejected. The SDK can log the hostname, so
+never place credentials in a hostname. CP verified failure diagnostics omit the
+substituted URL, headers and body; this is not a guarantee about all SDK debug logs.
+
+If a verified event receives 401/403, password refresh runs only when
+`auth.request` also declares `tls.ca_file`. Both the mint and the retry use their
+own verified transport. Put bearer tokens in headers. Verified failure logs omit
+substituted URLs, headers and bodies. Requests without `tls` retain their existing
+behavior. The catalog's shared legacy session does not support this opt-in and
+rejects manifests that declare `tls` on browse, download or auth requests/polls.
+
+This proposal depends on an SDK implementation that checks CA-load failures and
+matches the peer certificate to the requested hostname. The current SDK pin does
+not provide that contract. The paired SDK proposal exposes
+`SecureHttpClient::peerVerificationAvailable()`: true for the wolfSSL backend
+with both checks, false for an unsupported backend. This is a backend capability,
+not proof that a particular connection was verified. CP still supplies the CA,
+requires a fresh clock proof and rejects caller-owned sessions for each request.
+
+If the SDK has no capability method, CP builds but rejects verified requests
+before synchronizing the clock or opening a socket. It does not enable verification
+from a local build flag. The method and the SDK reference are proposals pending
+upstream acceptance; this patch does not advance the SDK gitlink.
